  "narrative": "**Narrative**\n\nThe differential metabolite list is strikingly enriched for the **Arachidonic Acid Metabolism** pathway (KEGG map00590; SMP00075), which returned the highest fold-enrichment (735-fold) with an FDR of 0.0 across six of the seven input compounds. Six metabolites — arachidonic acid, leukotriene A4, 15-HETE, 5-HETE, 11,12,15-THETA, and 15-deoxy-Δ12,14-PGJ2 — all map to this pathway, identifying it as the dominant affected system. The lone outlier, CDP (C00112), is a nucleotide intermediate unrelated to eicosanoid biology and likely reflects a separate, unconnected perturbation.\n\n**Key driver compounds and directional relationships.** Arachidonic acid (C00219) is the obligate upstream precursor. KEGG reaction-graph analysis confirms that arachidonic acid is directly connected (2 hops) to leukotriene A4 (C00909) via 5-HPETE (C05356), establishing the 5-lipoxygenase branch. Arachidonic acid is also upstream of 15-HETE (C04742) via an intermediate epoxide (C05966), confirming a second functional arm of the pathway activated in the treatment group.\n\n**Biological significance.** These changes collectively indicate a robust activation of the arachidonic acid cascade, engaging both the lipoxygenase (leukotriene and HETE) and cyclooxygenase (15-deoxy-PGJ2) branches. Elevated leukotriene A4 and 5-HETE are hallmarks of inflammatory cell recruitment and bronchoconstriction. The presence of 15-HETE and 11,12,15-THETA (a maresin/THETA-series mediator precursor) points toward specialized pro-resolving lipid mediator production. CDP elevation is mechanistically disconnected; its biological context requires independent investigation. These findings are consistent with an inflammatory or pro-resolving stimulus that broadly Reprograms the arachidonic acid metabolome.",
